# n8n
Workflow automation you can host yourself
14 Nov 2026

Shaun Chong

## What is n8n?

A workflow automation tool. You connect apps, APIs and AI models by drawing a
flow of nodes on a canvas, and n8n runs that flow whenever its trigger fires.

- Visual editor in the browser, with JavaScript or Python when the boxes are not enough
- Hundreds of built-in integrations, plus an HTTP Request node for any other API
- Self-host it for free, or pay for n8n Cloud
- Fair-code: the source is public under the Sustainable Use License

: Pronounced "n-eight-n", short for "nodemation". Started by Jan Oberhauser in Berlin in 2019.
: Think Zapier or Make, but you can run it on your own machine and read the code.

## The building blocks

Every workflow is a chain of nodes. Each node takes a list of JSON items in and
passes a list of JSON items out.

- **Triggers** start a run: a schedule, a webhook, a form, a chat message, an event in another app
- **Actions** do the work: Slack, Gmail, Google Sheets, Postgres, GitHub, HTTP Request
- **Logic** steers the data: If, Switch, Merge, Loop, Wait
- **Code** runs your own JavaScript or Python on the items
- **AI** nodes call a language model, or run an agent that can use other nodes as tools

: The JSON-items model is the main idea to get across: you can click any node after a run and see exactly what went in and what came out.

## Example: triaging support tickets

.html workflow.html

A ticket arrives at a webhook. A language model tags its category and urgency.
Urgent tickets page the on-call person in Slack; the rest get an acknowledgement
email.

: Without n8n this is a small service with a queue, an LLM client, a Slack client and an SMTP client. Here it is five nodes and some credentials.
: The If node branches on the field the AI node added, for example urgency equals "high".

## Triggering it from code

Anything that can send an HTTP request can start the workflow. Press Run.

.play webhook.go /^func main/,/^}/

: Needs n8n running locally with the Webhook node listening for a test event, path support-ticket, method POST. Otherwise Run prints the connection error.
: /webhook-test/ only works while the editor is listening. Once the workflow is active, callers use /webhook/ instead.

## What else people build with it

- **Reports**: every Monday, query a database and post a summary to Slack
- **Sync**: copy new CRM contacts into a spreadsheet or a mailing list
- **Alerts**: turn GitHub issues, failed deploys or uptime checks into chat messages
- **AI assistants**: a chat agent that answers from your docs and can create tickets
- **Glue**: move files between Drive, S3 and email without writing a service

: There are thousands of community templates at n8n.io/workflows. Importing one is a good way to learn the editor.

## Installing it: Docker

The quickest route. Docker keeps your workflows in a named volume.

    docker volume create n8n_data

    docker run -it --rm --name n8n -p 5678:5678 \
      -v n8n_data:/home/node/.n8n \
      docker.n8n.io/n8nio/n8n

Then open <http://localhost:5678>.

: --rm deletes the container when it stops, but the n8n_data volume keeps workflows and credentials.
: Optionally pass -e GENERIC_TIMEZONE and -e TZ so schedule triggers fire in your timezone.
: For a server, the docs recommend Docker Compose behind a reverse proxy for HTTPS.

## Installing it: Node.js

No Docker? n8n is also an npm package. It needs Node.js 20.19 to 24.x.

Try it once:

    npx n8n

Or install it:

    npm install -g n8n
    n8n start

Either way, the editor is at <http://localhost:5678>.

: On NixOS there is also a services.n8n module.

## Your first workflow

1. Open the editor and create the owner account
2. Add a trigger, for example a Webhook node
3. Add the nodes that should run after it, and connect your accounts when a node asks for credentials
4. Run it once from the editor and click each node to check its output
5. Activate the workflow so it runs on its own

: Credentials are stored encrypted in n8n, not in the workflow. Workflows themselves export as JSON, so they can go in git.

## When to reach for it

- Glue between services that would otherwise be a small, boring backend
- Teams where not everyone writes code, but someone can drop into a Code node
- Data that has to stay on your own machines

The licence lets you use it for your own work, including inside a company. It
does not let you resell n8n itself as a hosted service.

: Wrap up by showing the live editor if there is time.
